{#
    Account health: what is wrong, and the lightest thing that fixes it.

    Reads a HealthReport, already ordered worst-first with each consequence
    following its cause (HealthReport::arrange), so this file has no opinion
    about priority — it renders the list in the order it is given.

    Two rules the markup exists to keep:

      1. Every card says what the button will DO before you press it. The whole
         reason people delete an account and add it back is that "Reconnect"
         does not promise their mail will still be there, so the promise is on
         the page, not in a tooltip.

      2. Destructive repairs are visually separate and never the primary. They
         sit under a rule, in muted text, with the word that says what is lost.

      3. Every repair says what is happening the instant it is pressed, and
         stops accepting presses until that resolves. The POST repairs get this
         from `data-turbo-submits-with` — Turbo disables the submitter, swaps
         its label, and restores both on turbo:submit-end whether the request
         succeeded or failed. The reconnect LINK cannot use that idiom (it
         leaves for the provider, so there is no submission to instrument) and
         is handled by ui--health-repair instead.

         The pending labels are honest about what the press actually did. A
         resync DISPATCHES a message: "Starting sync…" is true, "Synced" would
         not be, and the card goes on saying it was started until the worker
         says otherwise — see the awaiting block below.
#}
{% import _self as health %}
<section id="settings-health" data-health-section
         data-controller="ui--health-repair"
         {# The words the controller writes. Nothing it renders is spelled in
            JavaScript; these are the whole of its vocabulary.

            `leaving` is the provider-LESS wording on purpose. Each reconnect
            link carries its own label in data-health-pending-label naming the
            brand it is about to send you to; this is only what a link without
            one falls back to, so it has no placeholder — there would be
            nothing to fill it with. #}
         data-ui--health-repair-i18n-value="{{ {
             leaving:      'settings.health.pending.reconnect_generic'|trans,
             synced:       'settings.health.outcome.calendar_synced'|trans,
             failedAgain:  'settings.health.outcome.calendar_failed_again'|trans,
             stillWaiting: 'settings.health.outcome.calendar_still_waiting'|trans,
         }|json_encode|e('html_attr') }}">
    {# THE ONE PAYBACK WRAPPER LEFT ON THIS PAGE, and it is the rule holding
       rather than an exception to it.

       Every other section is a single card and puts its heading in the card's
       header band, so it is inset by the band's own px-4 and needs no payback
       for the content wrapper's -mx-6. Health is not one card: it is a FEED of
       cards, one per issue, each carrying its own severity tint and its own
       indent when it is a consequence of the one above. Making this heading a
       card header would mean putting those cards inside a card, and flattening
       them into bands would cost the tint and the indent that stop one dead
       sign-in reading as nine separate emergencies.

       So the heading stays what the payback was written for — content that is
       not itself a card — and pays the -mx-6 back. See the rule as stated in
       settings/index.html.twig.

       IT PAYS AT EVERY WIDTH, and dropping the `@2xl:px-0` that used to be here
       is the fix rather than a regression. The heading and the cards below it
       are children of the same wrapper, so the -mx-6 moves both of them by the
       same 24px and cancelling it above @2xl moved only the heading. What was
       left lined the heading up with the cards' EDGE instead of their CONTENT:
       measured, the heading began 20px left of the first word under it on a
       desktop pane and 4px left of it below @2xl. Every other settings section
       puts its heading in a band and therefore starts it at the same x as the
       body — this one now does too. #}
    <div class="mb-4 px-4">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{{ 'settings.health.heading'|trans }}</h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mt-0.5">{{ 'settings.health.subheading'|trans }}</p>
    </div>

    {% if healthReport.isHealthy %}
        {#
            The healthy state is a real state and gets real words. A blank pane
            reads as "not loaded yet"; this reads as "checked, nothing wrong",
            which is the answer somebody who followed the topbar dot here after
            fixing something needs to see.
        #}
        {# px-4, the house inset from _partials/_card_chrome.html.twig, not the
           px-5 this used to carry. It is centred text, so the 4px never showed
           on its own — but the heading directly above it is inset by px-4, and
           a pane that starts its content one step further in is one more card
           on this page disagreeing with the rest. py-8 stays: this is an empty
           state, and the vertical air is what makes it read as an answer
           rather than as a card that failed to load. #}
        <div class="rounded-pane border border-line px-4 py-8 text-center" data-health-empty>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check text-2xl text-emerald-500 mb-3 block" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">{{ 'settings.health.healthy.title'|trans }}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mt-1 max-w-sm mx-auto">{{ 'settings.health.healthy.body'|trans }}</p>
        </div>
    {% else %}
        <div class="space-y-3">
            {% for issue in healthReport.issues %}
                {#
                    A consequence is indented and quieter than its cause. It is
                    still a card — the user's question really is "why is this
                    one calendar empty" — but it must not read as another
                    emergency, or one dead sign-in looks like nine problems.
                #}
                <div class="rounded-pane border border-line bg-surface {{ issue.severity.cardClasses }} {{ issue.isConsequence ? 'ml-0 @2xl:ml-6' : '' }}"
                     data-health-issue="{{ issue.id }}"
                     data-health-kind="{{ issue.kind.value }}"
                     data-health-severity="{{ issue.severity.value }}"
                     {% if issue.isConsequence %}data-health-caused-by="{{ issue.causedBy }}"{% endif %}>
                    {# Flat px-4. The `@2xl:px-5` that used to widen this was the
                       worst of the three insets on this page, because it only
                       appeared on a wide pane: every issue card quietly stepped
                       4px further in than the heading above the feed and than
                       every card in every other settings section, and only on
                       the screens most people use. py-4 rather than the macro's
                       py-5 is kept on purpose — these stack at space-y-3 and are
                       a feed of alerts, not one card's body. #}
                    <div class="px-4 py-4">
                        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                            <i class="fa-solid {{ issue.kind.icon }} mt-0.5 w-4 shrink-0 {{ issue.severity.iconClasses }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>

                            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
                                <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
                                    {{ issue.kind.titleKey|trans(issue.titleParams) }}
                                </h3>

                                {# What it means for their mail, in plain words. #}
                                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted mt-1 leading-relaxed">
                                    {{ issue.kind.bodyKey|trans(issue.bodyParams) }}
                                </p>

                                {#
                                    The dated evidence the verdict was read off.

                                    Above the repair, not behind the technical
                                    disclosure below it, and that placement is
                                    the point. The disclosure holds a provider's
                                    raw error string — something to search for,
                                    not something to reason with. These are the
                                    three dates that answer "is this lapsed or
                                    silent?", which is the question somebody
                                    was left unable to answer from the app at
                                    all, and an answer behind a click is an
                                    answer most people never see.

                                    Dates are formatted here rather than in the
                                    inspector because this is where the locale
                                    is known — see HealthFact.

                                    `Y-m-d H:i`, which is what every other dated
                                    row in settings uses (app passwords, push
                                    devices, calendars, the admin panel). These
                                    lines are read next to a scheduler log and a
                                    provider console, and a sortable unambiguous
                                    stamp is what that comparison needs — this
                                    is the one place a localised "13 Aug, 4:00
                                    am" would be harder to use, not easier.
                                #}
                                {% if issue.facts is not empty %}
                                    <dl class="mt-2.5 grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-[11px] leading-relaxed" data-health-facts>
                                        {% for fact in issue.facts %}
                                            <dt class="text-ink-faint">{{ fact.labelKey|trans(fact.labelParams) }}</dt>
                                            {# Only a deadline that has gone by
                                               is tinted — see HealthFact
                                               $alarmWhenPast. Every other date
                                               here is in the past by
                                               definition, and colouring those
                                               would make the colour meaningless
                                               on the card that most needs it to
                                               mean something. #}
                                            <dd class="{{ fact.isOverdue ? 'text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400 font-medium' : 'text-ink-muted' }}"
                                                data-health-fact="{{ fact.labelKey }}">
                                                {% if fact.isKnown %}
                                                    {{ fact.at|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}
                                                {% else %}
                                                    {{ fact.noneKey|trans }}
                                                {% endif %}
                                            </dd>
                                        {% endfor %}
                                    </dl>
                                {% endif %}

                                {% set safeRepairs = issue.safeRepairs %}
                                {% set riskyRepairs = issue.destructiveRepairs %}

                                {% if safeRepairs is not empty %}
                                    <div class="mt-3 space-y-2.5">
                                        {% for repair in safeRepairs %}
                                            <div>
                                                {# The promise, above the button that keeps it. #}
                                                <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mb-1.5">{{ repair.promiseKey|trans(repair.promiseParams) }}</p>

                                                {% if repair.isPost %}
                                                    {#
                                                        Wrapped, because on an awaiting card the
                                                        button starts hidden and something has to
                                                        hold the waiting line beside it.
                                                    #}
                                                    {% set awaiting = issue.isAwaiting and loop.first %}

                                                    {% if awaiting %}
                                                        {#
                                                            The press has already happened and the
                                                            worker has not answered. Saying "Try
                                                            syncing now" here would invite a second
                                                            dispatch of a message already queued;
                                                            saying "Synced" would be a lie, because
                                                            resyncCalendar only DISPATCHES. So the
                                                            card says the one thing that is true —
                                                            it has been started — and waits to be
                                                            told otherwise by
                                                            ui--health-repair#_onSyncFinished.
                                                        #}
                                                        <div data-ui--health-repair-target="awaiting"
                                                             data-health-awaiting
                                                             data-health-calendar-id="{{ repair.routeParams.id }}">
                                                            <p class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted"
                                                               role="status" aria-live="polite">
                                                                <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-notch fa-spin text-[11px]"
                                                                   data-health-spinner aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                                                <span data-health-awaiting-label>{{ issue.awaitingKey|trans }}</span>
                                                            </p>

                                                            {#
                                                                The way back out, rendered here and
                                                                hidden rather than built in
                                                                JavaScript: it carries a CSRF token
                                                                and a translated label, neither of
                                                                which a controller could produce.
                                                                Revealed when the sync reports a
                                                                failure, or when nothing has been
                                                                heard for long enough that claiming
                                                                to still be waiting would be the
                                                                stuck state rather than the truth.
                                                            #}
                                                            <div data-health-retry class="hidden mt-2">
                                                                {{ health.repairForm(repair) }}
                                                            </div>
                                                        </div>
                                                    {% else %}
                                                        {{ health.repairForm(repair) }}
                                                    {% endif %}
                                                {% else %}
                                                    {#
                                                        A link, not a form: this one leaves the
                                                        app for the provider's consent screen and
                                                        comes back. Nothing is written until it
                                                        does, so there is no state change here for
                                                        a token to protect — the write happens in
                                                        the callback, behind the state check and
                                                        the address guard.

                                                        data-turbo="false" means Turbo never sees a
                                                        submission, so data-turbo-submits-with has
                                                        nothing to instrument and the browser shows
                                                        nothing at all between the click and the
                                                        provider's page. ui--health-repair#leave
                                                        fills that gap and guards the second click.
                                                    #}
                                                    <a href="{{ path(repair.route, repair.routeParams) }}"
                                                       data-turbo="false"
                                                       data-ui--health-repair-target="leaving"
                                                       data-action="ui--health-repair#leave"
                                                       data-health-pending-label="{{ repair.pendingLabelKey|trans(repair.pendingParams) }}"
                                                       class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-accent px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent-ink hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity">
                                                        <span data-health-leave-label>{{ repair.labelKey|trans }}</span>
                                                        <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-up-right-from-square text-[10px]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                                    </a>
                                                {% endif %}
                                            </div>
                                        {% endfor %}
                                    </div>
                                {% endif %}

                                {% if issue.detail is not null %}
                                    {#
                                        The provider's own words, behind a
                                        disclosure. MailProvider::calendarScopes()
                                        makes the case that "invalid_grant" is not
                                        an explanation to put in front of a person
                                        — but a self-hoster matching this against a
                                        forum thread needs to be able to find it, so
                                        it is one click away rather than absent.
                                    #}
                                    <details class="mt-3 group">
                                        <summary class="text-xs text-ink-faint cursor-pointer hover:text-ink-muted select-none list-none inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
                                            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-right text-[9px] transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                            {{ 'settings.health.technical_detail'|trans }}
                                        </summary>
                                        <pre data-health-detail
                                             class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-raised px-3 py-2 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">{{ issue.detail }}</pre>
                                    </details>
                                {% endif %}

                                {% if riskyRepairs is not empty %}
                                    {#
                                        Under a rule and after the safe options,
                                        never beside them. These throw work away.
                                    #}
                                    <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-line">
                                        {% for repair in riskyRepairs %}
                                            <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mb-1.5">{{ repair.promiseKey|trans(repair.promiseParams) }}</p>
                                            {# data-turbo-confirm, matching every other
                                               destructive action in the app — see the
                                               admin failed-queue panel. #}
                                            <form method="post" action="{{ path(repair.route, repair.routeParams) }}"
                                                  data-turbo-confirm="{{ 'settings.health.confirm.destructive'|trans }}">
                                                <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token(repair.csrfTokenId) }}">
                                                {# The pending label is reached only AFTER the
                                                   confirm above has been answered — Turbo raises
                                                   the dialog before it starts the submission — so
                                                   this cannot make a purge easier to fire. It only
                                                   stops a second press of one already agreed to.
                                                   The button keeps its outline-and-danger styling
                                                   throughout, never the accent fill of the safe
                                                   repairs, so the pending state cannot make it
                                                   look like one of them. #}
                                                <button type="submit"
                                                        data-turbo-submits-with="{{ repair.pendingLabelKey|trans(repair.pendingParams) }}"
                                                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-line px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-danger hover:bg-hover transition-colors cursor-pointer disabled:cursor-progress disabled:opacity-70">
                                                    {{ repair.labelKey|trans }}
                                                </button>
                                            </form>
                                        {% endfor %}
                                    </div>
                                {% endif %}
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</section>

{#
    One POST repair, as a form with a token and a button that says what it is
    doing the instant it is pressed.

    A macro because it is rendered twice for the same repair on an awaiting
    card — once as the visible control, once hidden behind the waiting line as
    the way back out — and two copies of a form carrying a CSRF token is two
    places for the token id to drift.

    `data-turbo-submits-with` goes on the BUTTON, not the form, and that is not
    a preference: Turbo reads the attribute off the submitter only (see
    FormSubmission#submitsWith), so on the form it is silently ignored. Turbo
    disables the submitter for the duration and restores both label and state on
    turbo:submit-end — including when the request errors, which is what keeps a
    failed repair from leaving a dead button behind.
#}
{% macro repairForm(repair) %}
    <form method="post" action="{{ path(repair.route, repair.routeParams) }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token(repair.csrfTokenId) }}">
        <button type="submit"
                data-turbo-submits-with="{{ repair.pendingLabelKey|trans(repair.pendingParams) }}"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-accent px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent-ink hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity cursor-pointer disabled:cursor-progress disabled:opacity-70">
            {{ repair.labelKey|trans }}
        </button>
    </form>
{% endmacro %}
